A DEFINITION IS DROPPED FROM AN ITEM THAT KEEPS ITS TEXT.

WHAT md DOES TODAY. The item survives and the definition does not:

    - Some text

WHAT IT SHOULD DO. Write the definition back at the item's content indent,
where the author put it.

WHY THIS IS NOT THE SAME CASE AS ITS NEIGHBOUR.
`format-keeps-a-link-reference-definition-in-a-list-item` has an item whose
ONLY content was the definition, so the item ends up with no children and the
empty-item machinery decides its fate. Here the item has text, `md blocks`
already says `1 items`, and nothing about emptiness is involved at any point.
The item is written correctly and the definition simply never reaches the
writer.

That means a fix aimed only at empty items cannot help this shape, and a
reader who finds the neighbour first should not assume it covers this.

THIS IS THE QUIETEST OF THE THREE. The top level case loses a definition from
a document that otherwise looks untouched. The definition-only item at least
leaves a bare `-` behind as a hint that something was there. Here the output
is a perfectly ordinary list item, and nothing at all suggests a URL was
deleted.

CAUSE. The same one, and it is not a cmark bug. `[ref]: /url` is a link
reference DEFINITION, a block level construct that by design renders nothing;
cmark consumes it into the document's reference map and leaves no node, so
`MarkdownParser.parse` never sees it. See
`format-keeps-an-unused-link-reference-definition`, which owns the cause, and
whose fix — reading definitions out of the SOURCE before the tree is walked —
is what this case needs too, plus knowing which item the definition sat in so
it goes back inside the bullet rather than after the list.
